TOPOLOGY AS TESTED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  One port, two crafted client MACs at the same time, and two VLANs built for this case
  so that each client has a gateway of its own to reach.  The DUT is both the
  authenticator and the RADIUS server, so no second switch and no external server took
  part.

        tb277  eth4   -- S1 02:0d:b9:00:33:62 as ckdv1, sender IP 10.38.100.50
             |           S2 02:0d:b9:00:33:63 as ckdv2, sender IP 10.38.101.50
             |           EAPOL from tools/eapol.py, ARP from tools/arpleg.py
             |
             |  (down)  EAPOL frames, then ARP requests for one VLAN's gateway
             |  (up)    ARP replies, but only from the client's own assigned VLAN
             v
    +--------------------------------------------------------+
    |  swi_d   AT-x960-56YSQ                       <-- DUT   |
    |  port1.0.48: dot1x port-control auto,                  |
    |              auth host-mode multi-supplicant,          |
    |              auth dynamic-vlan-creation type multi,    |
    |              auth multi-vlan-session                   |
    |                                                        |
    |  interface vlan362 10.38.100.1/24   <-- S1's gateway   |
    |  interface vlan363 10.38.101.1/24   <-- S2's gateway   |
    |  interface vlan48  10.38.22.66/27   <-- the port's own |
    |                                         access VLAN    |
    |         |                                              |
    |         +--> radius-server local, 127.0.0.1            |
    |                group ckdvg1 -> vlan 362 -> user ckdv1  |
    |                group ckdvg2 -> vlan 363 -> user ckdv2  |
    +--------------------------------------------------------+
             ^
             |  console, 115200 baud
        tb277  /dev/u2

  DUT     : swi_d (AT-x960-56YSQ) console u2 at 115200 baud, S/N A2WQS700B,
            software sin_x96056-20261019-27.  Not a stack.
  server  : the DUT's own local RADIUS server.  `radius-server local`, NAS 127.0.0.1.
            The local server has no per-user `vlan` keyword, so the VLAN is carried by
            a group: `group <name>` then `vlan <id>`, with the user placed in the group.
  link    : tb277 eth4 <-> swi_d port1.0.48 (access port in vlan 48; the DUT's vlan 48
            address is 10.38.22.66/27, MAC e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43)
  clients : S1 MAC 02:0d:b9:00:33:62, identity ckdv1, group ckdvg1, VLAN 362
            S2 MAC 02:0d:b9:00:33:63, identity ckdv2, group ckdvg2, VLAN 363
  probe   : the dataplane probe is 3 ARP requests for one VLAN's gateway address, sent
            with a sender IP inside that same subnet.  So the only thing that differs
            between a leg that reaches and a leg that is blocked is which VLAN the
            untagged frame landed in.  The measurement is how many ARP replies come
            back.
  tools   : tools/eapol.py and tools/arpleg.py on tb277; tools/tb ckcon for the
            console; tools/qmark.py for the read-only help probe; tools/tb health for
            the two health reads

  Built for this case and removed again at the end: vlan 362 `ckDYNVLAN-A` with
  `interface vlan362 10.38.100.1/24`; vlan 363 `ckDYNVLAN-B` with
  `interface vlan363 10.38.101.1/24`; local RADIUS groups ckdvg1 and ckdvg2; local
  RADIUS users ckdv1 and ckdv2.  Both subnets were unused.  vlan 60, vlan 10 and
  vlan 48 were not touched.

  Two clients on one port need `auth host-mode multi-supplicant`.  Sending that command
  changed `dot1x control-direction both` to `dot1x control-direction in`, with no
  warning and no `% ` line.  The setting was restored explicitly at the end of the case
  and read back.

  Every leg below was preceded by `dot1x initialize interface port1.0.48`.

